# Explore Reversible Elementary Cellular Automata

Begin with the central question: when does a nearest-neighbor binary law preserve every bit of global information? Use the laboratory below to test any of the $256$ rules on finite cycles.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The exhaustive classification algorithm explains what the widget computes.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the mathematical definition of reversibility</summary>
For a cycle of length $n$, a configuration is $x\in\{0,1\}^n$, and the global map is $F_{w,n}(x)_i=f_w(x_{i-1},x_i,x_{i+1})$. Reversibility means that $F_{w,n}$ is bijective. Learn more about [cellular automata](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellular_automaton) and [bijective functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bijection).
</details>

## Watch the candidate set collapse

Successive tests at lengths $1,2,3,4$ leave exactly six rules. The visualization and complete scan make the finite argument tangible.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the short-period obstruction theorem</summary>
Every rule outside $\{15,51,85,170,204,240\}$ fails bijectivity on at least one cycle of length at most four. A repeated output gives two distinct past configurations with the same future.
</details>

## Meet the six survivors

The six rules are shifts, complement, identity, and complemented shifts. Edit a configuration and apply them directly.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the inverse proof</summary>
Left and right cyclic shifts are mutual inverses. Complement is an involution and commutes with shifts. Therefore all six maps possess explicit inverses for every positive cycle length. The generated transformations connect naturally to [cyclic groups](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group) and [reversible computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reversible_computing).
</details>

## Find where information is lost

Pick an excluded rule and request a concrete collision.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal complexity and scaling</summary>
Testing one rule on an $n$-cycle costs $O(n2^n)$ time by direct enumeration. Testing all $256$ rules through length four is tiny, but larger radii motivate [de Bruijn graphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Bruijn_graph), which encode neighborhood overlap more economically.
</details>

## Dynamics in motion

Rule $170$ is pure transport: each row is a cyclic shift of the preceding row.

{{visualization:2}}

